      {
        "id": 23275631,
        "name": "CutiePi",
        "username": "CutiePi-2727634",
        "avatar_template": "/en/wow/plugins/discourse-blizzard-plugin/images/avatars/wow/default.png",
        "created_at": "2024-04-02T18:27:45.297Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"anon63184095\" data-post=\"1\" data-topic=\"1821486\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://d38bqls1q93fod.cloudfront.net/en/wow/plugins/discourse-blizzard-plugin/images/avatars/wow/default.png\" class=\"avatar\"> anon63184095:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Do you think that machine learning could be used to stop moving players to other shards when they are in the middle of a multi-step zone event?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I think maybe a simple if statement could do that. If(scenario mode not active){do sharding method or whatever it is}</p>\n<p>Machine learning is often overkill, if you can do it yourself with 100% accuracy, there’s no need to create and train a model with possibly lower accuracy.</p>",
